00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:06.789 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello everybody  and greetings from an unexpectedly sunny  spring day in germany  thank you very much for the kind  invitation my name is thomas metzinger  and i'm a professor of philosophy at the  university of mainz in germany  in these two presentations today i want  to try and  make you see something  you may not have seen before in current  philosophy there are two hot  topics namely the ethics the applied  ethics of artificial intelligence  and the problem of consciousness what i  want to try  today is if you can see the connection  between these two  fields of research  so in this first short talk i want to  talk about the problem  of artificial consciousness and give a  brief sketch of an argument  why we should be very careful  with implementing this  so from a 2018 to  20 i have worked in the european  commission's high level expert group on  artificial intelligence  i have co-authored the ethic guidelines  for trustworthy ai  and the policy and investment  recommendations for trustworthy  ai and it was a very interesting  experience for me i met very many smart  people  52 top level experts on ai  and i definitely learned a lot we  published our  guidelines on the 11th of april 2019 and  the policy  and investment recommendations uh on the  26th  of june however there are some bad  news these ethics guidelines were  strongly  contaminated by industrial lobby  and actually from a philosopher's point  of view  they are a lot of hot air there's no  real  normative substance there are no real  concrete recommendations and  applications and for example all red  lines  have been taken out of the final  document  i think they are also short-sighted  because they deliberately ignore some  long-term issues and here are some  examples  of issues the high-level group  deliberately ignored the problem of  artificial general intelligence the  problem  of possible possibly autonomous moral  agents  emerging and the problem i want to talk  about today  artificial consciousness or as it is  sometimes called  synthetic phenomenology alluding to  synthetic biology so here's the first  technical term  uh you have to learn i i understand some  of you  have to give go through a test at the  end of this course  so the new term is ethics washing  this is if you organize and cultivate  ethical  debates because you want to delay  postpone avoid or deter  people from policy making and  regulation so there are people who have  an interest  in these ethics debates going on  just to buy themselves time  i think what we really need and what we  urgently need  is binding rules which are also  enforceable  and which are legitimate because there  is a full democratic  process behind them  the good news however is that the  current  european ethics guidelines are much  better than anything  china or the united states actually have  and those current ethics guidelines are  also an excellent  first step a plateau to begin a second  phase of discussion and to develop them  further so what we can actually do is we  can move the discussion back  from a place where it is dominated by  industrial lobby  which just wants to decorate a major  investment strategy  by making it look ethical  into a more constructive and  intellectually honest context  maybe that is here but i will confine  myself to one single topic today  synthetic phenomenology i'm one of the  guys  that founded the association for the  scientific study of consciousness  27 years ago and i've been  working in the consciousness community  for  30 years of my life there's something  that is slightly beginning  to make me nervous there are four labs  now  on this planet with esteemed colleagues  of mine people who i really like  and take them uh really seriously who  basically say  we want to create artificial  consciousness and we're gonna do it as  soon as we know we can do it  so here you have for instance a  statement from stan dehain and his group  in in paris and they want a confluence  of ai  and the cognitive neuroscience of  consciousness  but you also have in tokyo for instance  ryota kanai there's naotsugria  in melbourne and here's the typical  american  way of putting the point michael  graziano from princeton  also a very well-known consciousness  researcher  one could build it for entertainment  value  it would be monumentally cool  i'm not so sure so my main  thesis is a political demand we should  impose a global 30-year moratorium on  all research that knowingly risks or  directly aims  at the creation of artificial  consciousness  and i think there should be a worldwide  ban  on synthetic phenomenology until 2050  or until we know what we are doing  so what is the risk description what do  we want to avoid  i think we could create conscious  suffering on non-biological carrier  systems we could have negative  phenomenology  possibly in a very large number of  individuals think of cascades of copies  or cascades of virtual agents  i call this the explosion of negative  phenomenology  it's not an intelligence explosion but  an explosion of artificial suffering  but there's a problem of epistemic  indeterminacy and i'm going to explain  in a second what this means  we might not even be aware of it because  we don't have a theory of consciousness  basically we don't know what we are  doing in this field  at all so here  is from an article which i'll come back  to later that  will be published in about a week or two  weeks and which will be freely available  for everybody um what is  epistemic indeterminacy  this means that it is not the case  that either we know that artificial  consciousness will  emerge at some point all that we know  that artificial consciousness will never  be instantiated on machines  it is this neonas i think that we have  to deal with in an  ethically sensitive way and in a way  that's evidence-based rational  and intellectually honest and this is  also an important point  i am not saying i have been in this  field for 30 years  this will happen tomorrow and i don't  believe  that it will happen the day after  tomorrow  but my point is that it doesn't matter  what i'm actually thinking  the the problem is this indeterminacy  not that i'm making a statement towards  probability  so let's have a look at the applied  ethics for ai  my background assumption is that what  gives an entity moral status  is the capacity for conscious suffering  and  that in this world reducing suffering  must always have greater priority than  increasing happiness  i'm firmly convinced that there's more  suffering  than joy in this world a brief aside  ai and robot ethics have has a lot of  strong parallels to animal ethics  take this classical quote from arthur  schopenhauer the assumption that animals  are without rights and the illusion  that our treatment of them has no moral  significance  is a positively outrageous example of  western crudity and barbarity  universal compassion is the only  guarantee of morality  so please note that tomorrow's  self-conscious machines have no  representation in  any ethics committee on this planet or  in any political process  but we need to know what that thing is  what is suffering  very simple this uh definition would be  any kind of state the system would  avoid if it had a chance  massive prediction errors in the system  self model  in particular for negative effect  unexpectedly decreasing rates  of prediction error minimization  loss of self-control and negative  balances negatively valued states  in the system's conscious self model  so what i want to do now is i want to  briefly give you  four necessary conditions  for what might be to continue watching  this video  click the link in the top left or in the  description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas

